  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "Lanmaoa carminipes is a striking bolete fungus characterized by its distinctive red or crimson-staining stem, which is the most recognizable feature of this species. Found in New England forests, this fungus forms mycorrhizal relationships with trees and appears during the warmer months of the year.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Crimson Staining Stem",
      "description": "The stem (stipe) displays bright red or carmine coloration, especially at the base, which intensifies when bruised or handled",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Cap and Pore Surface",
      "description": "The cap is typically brown to tan, while the pore surface underneath is yellow to olive-yellow and may bruise greenish-blue",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Spore Print",
      "description": "Produces an olive-brown to olive-yellow spore print, which helps confirm identification",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Lanmaoa carminipes is found in mixed hardwood and coniferous forests of New England, typically fruiting on the forest floor in association with oak, birch, and other trees during summer and early fall.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "The carmine-red coloring of the stem serves as a visual beacon in the forest, making this bolete one of the more easily spotted species among the often-cryptic fungal fruiting bodies on the woodland floor.",
